WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.520
What on Earth does Robert De Niro have to do with&nbsp;
the Ghostbusters? And what's with all the branded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.640
snacks? "Ghostbusters: Afterlife" is an Easter&nbsp;
egg lover's dream — here are some of the best.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
We learn in "Afterlife" that before he died,&nbsp;
Dr. Egon Spengler lived in a dilapidated old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:20.000
farm house in Summerville, Oklahoma, the locals&nbsp;
mockingly called him "the dirt farmer," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.120
he died deeply in debt. He also seems to&nbsp;
have been obsessed with Revelation 6:12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.520
as he has excerpts and references to the&nbsp;
bible verse spray painted around the grounds.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.120
Further tying the old friends together,&nbsp;
when Phoebe calls Ray Stantz, you may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.520
noticed the former Ghostbuster is sporting&nbsp;
a new tattoo on his arm. That tattoo on Ray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:44.240
reads ... you guessed it ... Revelation 6:12. In&nbsp;
the King James bible, that verse goes like this:

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.080
"And I beheld when he had opened the sixth&nbsp;
seal, and, lo, there was a great earthquake.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.320
And the sun became black as sackcloth&nbsp;
of hair, and the moon became as blood."

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.440
In most movies, this could be called&nbsp;
"foreshadowing." But if there's such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.520
a thing as "backwards-shadowing," this might&nbsp;
qualify, because the verse clearly calls back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.520
to the day of judgment on Stantz's mind in&nbsp;
the original "Ghostbusters" film when Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:08.000
Venkman told the mayor that New York was headed&nbsp;
towards a disaster of biblical proportions.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.800
"What he means is Old Testament, Mr.&nbsp;
Mayor, real wrath of God-type stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.400
Fire and brimstone coming down from&nbsp;
the skies! Rivers and seas boiling!"

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.880
Fun fact: In the original "Ghostbusters,"&nbsp;
there is a scene where Ray recites the bible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.480
verse to Winston — but he mistakenly cites&nbsp;
it as Revelation 7:12 instead of Chapter 6.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.400
As we observe the home that Egon died in recently,&nbsp;
the dilapidated residence screams a few things:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.840
eccentric genius, ghost hunter and dude who lived&nbsp;
alone, most significantly. But, like anyone of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.800
retiree age, there are lots of references to&nbsp;
his old job — and an unwillingness to let it go.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.280
The equipment is most obvious. Egon’s grandkids&nbsp;
find a ghost trap buried in the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.000
a P.K.E. meter in the main home, and a&nbsp;
proton pack and the old Ecto-1 vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.080
also on the grounds. Beyond that, we see odd&nbsp;
stacks of books arranged similar to what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.240
Ghostbusters found when they had their first&nbsp;
ghost encounter at New York City Public Library.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.640
"No human being would stack books like this."

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.800
In Egon’s lab, there are also numerous references&nbsp;
to his ‘80s NYC heyday. To enter, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.520
you have to slide down a fireman’s pole much like&nbsp;
the one Ray once treasured. Under some lights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.080
we see what looks to be the “spores, molds and&nbsp;
fungus” collection that Egon told Janine about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.520
and then we see a closet filled&nbsp;
with old Ghostbusters uniforms.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.480
When the kids put on the uniforms,&nbsp;
we know they scribble their own names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.840
where the nametag typically goes.&nbsp;
When the kids explore the pockets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.280
they also find evidence that Egon&nbsp;
hasn’t emptied them in some time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.520
In addition to a pair of his old glasses, they&nbsp;
find a Nestle Crunch wrapper — referencing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.520
long-discussed product placement moment at&nbsp;
the beginning of the original “Ghostbusters”

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.840
"Egon, I'm going to take back some&nbsp;
of the things I've said about you."

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.720
"You’ve … you've earned it."

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.680
The kids later discover a very old looking&nbsp;
Twinkie in the glovebox of the Etco-1. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.640
is undoubtedly a reference to Egon’s “Big Twinkie”&nbsp;
speech from the original film. It’s not the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.840
Twinkie — it’s unwrapped in the old movie, and we&nbsp;
see Egon take a bite — but that’s an old Twinkie.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.560
Although “Afterlife” concerns itself with&nbsp;
three generations and plenty of characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.280
certain matters of the heart&nbsp;
seem to be conveniently omitted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.320
while others are only alluded&nbsp;
to. Most noticeable, perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.560
is the super-sweet Egon/Janine relationship&nbsp;
we all loved from the original film.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:22.640
It's reasonable to assume that their relationship&nbsp;
fizzled out sometime in the early ‘90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.400
as the Ghostbusters business did the same.&nbsp;
It’s also reasonable to assume that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.560
Egon and Janine began flirting with each&nbsp;
other, his daughter Callie was already born.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.160
So, while none of this is canon, one&nbsp;
could draw the conclusion that Egon had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.080
already been married and divorced before the&nbsp;
events of the original “Ghostbusters” film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.960
which provides some interesting&nbsp;
new shading to the character.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.360
Callie doesn’t discuss her mother, and actress&nbsp;
Carrie Coon could reasonably pass for someone a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.680
few years younger than her actual age of 40.&nbsp;
Could it be that in an “Afterlife” sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.080
Janine would reveal that she&nbsp;
is Callie’s birth mother?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.160
Probably not, but if so, remember&nbsp;
that you heard it here first.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.640
On much more firm footing, the mid-credits&nbsp;
scene delightfully comes after the credits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.680
list Sigourney Weaver, who had&nbsp;
not yet appeared in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.320
When the screen lights up, we see the “Aliens”&nbsp;
series star sitting at a table opposite Venkman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.960
playing the old psychic card game test that&nbsp;
Peter once employed to hit on college co-eds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.120
The pair seem extremely playful, and extremely&nbsp;
comfortable with each other — it’s the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:21.120
movie chemistry that only works when you put&nbsp;
two personalities together and they click,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.360
and it’s a key element Weaver and Murray&nbsp;
brought to the first two “Ghostbusters” films.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.040
It's also, perhaps, the dynamic you&nbsp;
might expect from an old married couple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.640
Although it isn’t clearly stated, the scene&nbsp;
would seem to indicate that Peter Venkman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:36.560
eventually settled down and put a ring on&nbsp;
the cello-plucking finger of Dana Barrett.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.120
Now, that would have been a fun&nbsp;
wedding reception to attend.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.000
The name “Ivo Shandor” might not mean much&nbsp;
to some most heading into “Afterlife,” but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.400
by the end of the film it will. As briefly&nbsp;
discussed in the original “Ghostbusters” film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.000
Shandor was a cultist who designed&nbsp;
the 550 Central Park West building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.960
that eventually became ground&nbsp;
zero for the Gozer invasion.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.280
"The architect's name was Ivo Shandor.&nbsp;
I found it in Tobin's Spirit Guide."

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.600
By employing certain materials in the building’s&nbsp;
construction, he made it conducive to spectral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.760
inhabitation — and in the years since Ivan&nbsp;
Reitman’s films, Shandor’s backstory has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.720
been significantly expanded upon by media&nbsp;
like 2009’s “Ghostbusters: The Video Game."

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.720
References in “Afterlife” begin with the&nbsp;
name of the new ground zero in Summerville.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.560
In the first scene of the film, you can see&nbsp;
Egon’s car crashing through gates that read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:26.480
“Shandor Mining Co., Est. 1927,” an entrance&nbsp;
that can be seen several other times as well.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.720
When the new generation of Ghostbusters begins&nbsp;
exploring the mine, one of the things they find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:36.640
down there is the corpse of Ivo Shandor, played&nbsp;
by J.K. Simmons, lying in state in a glass coffin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.320
Podcast finds this endlessly creepy&nbsp;
— but even more so when it becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.560
clear that Shandor is being resurrected&nbsp;
alongside his beloved Gozer the Gozerian.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.960
When it becomes apparent that Phoebe and Trevor&nbsp;
have some research to do on the grandfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.040
they never met and his old life, they go&nbsp;
precisely where any modern teenager would:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.200
the card catalog at their local library.&nbsp;
Just kidding — of course, it’s YouTube.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:02.480
For starters, teacher/seismologist Mr. Grooberson&nbsp;
shows them clips of the so-called “Manhattan ghost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.800
stories” he explains to them when he first sees&nbsp;
the ghost trap. Later, Phoebe goes on YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.280
again to watch the old Ghostbusters commercial.&nbsp;
While all that footage is glimpsed in the 1984&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.760
“Ghostbusters” movie, the film cleverly&nbsp;
re-purposes it as a historical artifact.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.360
This is particularly effective because&nbsp;
the climactic street scenes at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.600
of the original “Ghostbusters” effectively&nbsp;
captured the chaos of the gathering crowd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:28.000
as well as a shoot that feels unplanned;&nbsp;
looking back now, it seems like news footage.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.200
The commercial, meanwhile, was particularly funny&nbsp;
because Murray, Aykroyd and Ramis are acting like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.360
inexperienced public speakers conveying the&nbsp;
awkward line readings and none-too-subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:41.920
attempts to hit their marks that you might&nbsp;
have seen in an ’80s low-budget commercial. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.072
and the phone number in that old commercial? In&nbsp;
“Afterlife,” Phoebe calls it and gets Ray Stantz.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.120
In one of the film’s more random comments,&nbsp;
when Ray Stantz is running through the events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.120
of the last thirty years to catch up Egon's&nbsp;
granddaughter, he references a movie star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.800
as having contributed to the downfall of&nbsp;
the Ghostbusters. When he drops the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.920
you can imagine Robert De Niro&nbsp;
innocently sitting in the theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.120
getting all offended and screaming at&nbsp;
the screen: “You talkin’ about me?”

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.720
During the exposition dump,&nbsp;
Stantz remarks that, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.080
“Some actor bought up most of&nbsp;
Tribeca, and we lost the firehouse.”

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.720
This most certainly refers to De Niro,&nbsp;
who founded TriBeCa productions in 1989&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:21.360
(just when things would have begun going south&nbsp;
for the Ghostbusters) with partner Jane Rosenthal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.400
Together, they have since organized&nbsp;
the annual Tribeca film Festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:28.880
and De Niro also opened the restaurant&nbsp;
Tribeca Grill in 1990. The “Taxi Driver”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.920
legend has also at various points had a stake in&nbsp;
Tribeca’s Greenwich Hotel, the Tribeca Cinemas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.880
and as of 2018 his “business empire” was&nbsp;
worth an estimated $1 billion dollars.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.840
Although Aykroyd and De Niro have&nbsp;
never appeared in a film together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:46.480
the line feels like an ad-lib lovingly teasing&nbsp;
De Niro’s good fortune in business — and how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:50.800
that firehouse property would likely be worth&nbsp;
today if the Ghostbusters had held onto it.

00:07:51.328 --> 00:07:55.760
The "Gary Grooberson goes to Walmart" scene&nbsp;
is a plethora of Easter eggs and callbacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.400
many referencing the adventures of Louis&nbsp;
Tully. While the somewhat-retired Rick Moranis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.080
is pretty much the only “Ghostbusters”&nbsp;
star to not come back for “Afterlife,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.160
the film essentially turns Paul Rudd’s&nbsp;
character into a modern-day equivalent.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.240
In Walmart, Grooberson has a normal scene&nbsp;
in public turn into a horrifying moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.160
of isolation — reminiscent of Tully’s&nbsp;
experience at the Tavern on the Green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.480
First he spots tiny marshmallow men coming&nbsp;
to life (the so-called “Mini-Pufts”),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.240
adorable at first and then turning into&nbsp;
Gremlins-like creatures who take pleasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.280
in turning each other into s’mores. Then he&nbsp;
stumbles upon Vinz Clortho, the demigod Tully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.840
thought was a dog — appropriately enough,&nbsp;
with its head buried in a bag of dog food.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:38.000
Like Louis Tully, Grooberson is cornered&nbsp;
and transformed into the Keymaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.600
Another great touch is in the scenes where&nbsp;
Grooberson is possessed — and Rudd is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.160
undoubtedly running with the same unmistakable&nbsp;
gait that Moranis employed in the original film.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.080
As much fun as it is to watch the&nbsp;
new generation busting ghosts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:54.640
let’s be honest: The film’s money moment is&nbsp;
seeing the original four Ghostbusters (okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.680
three and a CG-powered recreation of Harold&nbsp;
Ramis as a ghost) once again battling Gozer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.520
The entire scene is packed with references&nbsp;
to the classic showdown on the rooftop of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:07.920
550 Central Park West, and some Easter&nbsp;
eggs are easier to spot than others.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.760
Perhaps the funniest is after Ray&nbsp;
tries once again to make Gozer vacate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.840
the premises by identifying himself as a&nbsp;
representative of local law enforcement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.800
The Gozerian replies: “Are you a god?”

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:22.320
Of course, in the original film, Ray&nbsp;
answers “no,” resulting in the group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.880
nearly being killed by a&nbsp;
blast from its fingertips.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.320
"Ray, when someone asks you&nbsp;
if you’re a god, you say yes!"

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.040
In “Afterlife,” Ray wisely looks to his&nbsp;
partners for help with the question.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.560
The final big Easter eggs in "Afterlife" might be&nbsp;
planting seeds to pay off in other "Ghostbusters"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.400
movies, rather than this one. By the end of&nbsp;
the film, it becomes clear that Callie and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.120
kids are going to relocate to NYC and continue&nbsp;
their ghostbusting efforts where it all began.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:52.080
Their efforts will be funded by&nbsp;
none other than Winston Zeddemore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.920
who the franchise seems to be setting up as&nbsp;
a Bruce Wayne-like benefactor who will gladly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.080
throw an endless amount of money behind&nbsp;
re-establishing the Ghostbusters. He says,

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.280
"I want to be an example of what's&nbsp;
possible. I may be a businessman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:05.360
but I will always be a ghostbuster."

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.240
We see Winston re-purchasing the old firehouse,&nbsp;
and even get a glimpse of the blinking red light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:15.520
on the dusty old containment unit. Worth noting:&nbsp;
the red status light traditionally indicates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:19.840
there's a ghost in the unit. Could Slimer&nbsp;
be in there? We'll have to wait and see.

